============================================================
ABSOLUTE OLD-WORLD RULE
============================================================

The world currently stored under:

ServerStorage.NOT IN USE.GameWorld

was deliberately rejected and moved there because its visual quality,
composition, terrain, forest, scale and overall result were unacceptable.

It is ARCHIVE / REFERENCE ONLY.

DO NOT:
- move it back to Workspace;
- clone it into Workspace;
- rename it and call it new;
- use its terrain composition as the new world;
- use its forest composition as the new world;
- perform a cosmetic polish pass on it;
- reactivate it through Rojo;
- treat previous PASS checkpoints as visual approval.

OLD_REJECTED_WORLD_REUSED must ultimately equal NO.

You may inspect it only when necessary to understand gameplay contracts or
identify things that must NOT be reproduced.

============================================================
PRIMARY OBJECTIVE
============================================================

Create a genuinely NEW, LARGE, HIGH-QUALITY creepy survival world based on:

1. the approved visual reference / references;
2. the intended BunkerGame survival-horror vision;
3. approved reusable assets in the Asset Library;
4. existing gameplay contracts that must remain functional.

The finished environment should feel intentionally art-directed rather than
procedurally scattered.

The player should look across the environment and perceive a believable,
large survival landscape — not a collection of Roblox Parts placed on a
flat baseplate.

============================================================
MANDATORY ORCHESTRATION
============================================================

Muse is Director.

Muse must actually orchestrate the established pipeline rather than merely
describing it.

Use, where applicable:

MUSE
→ QWEN
→ MIMO
→ ROBLOX ASSISTANT
→ BLOXFORGE
→ MUSE VISUAL REVIEW

Responsibilities:

MUSE:
- art direction;
- world direction;
- visual standards;
- composition decisions;
- rejection/revision;
- final visual review.

QWEN:
- reasoning;
- reference analysis;
- world topology;
- biome/layout reasoning;
- composition planning;
- technical planning where useful.

MIMO:
- execution orchestration;
- coordinate actual tool calls;
- coordinate Roblox Assistant and BloxForge.

ROBLOX ASSISTANT:
- native Roblox creative generation;
- environment composition;
- terrain/native generation where appropriate;
- visual asset generation where appropriate;
- reference-driven generation where appropriate.

BLOXFORGE:
- engineering;
- technical validation;
- filesystem persistence;
- Rojo-safe integration;
- geometry checks;
- collision;
- pivot/orientation;
- scale;
- performance;
- scripts;
- gameplay integration;
- QA;
- Asset Library registration.

Never claim that an agent/tool participated unless it was actually invoked.

Record real invocation/session/request IDs whenever available.

============================================================
REFERENCE-FIRST RULE
============================================================

The approved visual reference is the visual target.

Do not replace it with an invented generic Roblox aesthetic.

Study the reference BEFORE major composition.

Extract from it:

- terrain language;
- vegetation density;
- tree silhouette;
- spacing;
- elevation;
- path language;
- rock distribution;
- landmark hierarchy;
- atmospheric depth;
- lighting;
- palette;
- foreground/midground/background layering;
- scale relationships;
- visual noise;
- negative space;
- horror/survival mood.

The result does not need to copy the reference literally.

It DOES need to achieve comparable visual coherence and environmental
believability.

If a visual decision contradicts the reference without a gameplay or
technical reason, reconsider it.

============================================================
ASSET LIBRARY FIRST
============================================================

Before generating assets, inspect the approved BunkerGame Asset Library.

Reuse approved assets/families whenever they are sufficient.

For a required variation:

1. find the closest approved library asset/family;
2. use it as the visual/reference base when appropriate;
3. generate/edit through the established native workflow;
4. BloxForge technical QA;
5. Muse visual approval;
6. register the approved reusable variant in the Asset Library.

Do not register:
- temporary assets;
- rejected assets;
- test assets;
- broken assets.

Do not generate dozens of expensive unique assets when a small coherent
reusable kit can achieve a better result.

============================================================
WORLD SCALE
============================================================

The new world must feel LARGE.

It must support:
- exploration;
- survival resource gathering;
- navigation;
- tension;
- long sightlines;
- controlled sightline breaks;
- landmarks;
- forest traversal;
- combat;
- bunker discovery;
- future expansion.

Do not create a tiny decorative arena around the bunker.

Use natural distance and terrain to make different regions feel spatially
distinct.

============================================================
TERRAIN
============================================================

Create genuinely convincing terrain.

Avoid:
- giant obvious rectangular ground slabs;
- repetitive wedge hills;
- evenly spaced hills;
- visible artificial terrain seams;
- identical elevation everywhere;
- a flat world with decorative bumps.

Use:
- rolling elevation;
- ridges;
- valleys;
- depressions;
- natural slopes;
- berms;
- terrain occlusion;
- elevated overlooks;
- lower traversal corridors;
- irregular silhouettes;
- terrain transitions;
- natural bunker embedment.

Terrain must help direct exploration.

The bunker should feel BUILT INTO the landscape, not dropped on top of it.

============================================================
FOREST
============================================================

Create a believable forest system.

Do NOT make:
- grid forests;
- evenly spaced trees;
- identical tree rotation;
- identical scale;
- identical species everywhere;
- obvious procedural circles.

Use density gradients.

Include:
- dense forest;
- medium forest;
- sparse transitional zones;
- clearings;
- disturbed vegetation near human infrastructure;
- forest-edge composition;
- dead/fallen vegetation where appropriate;
- natural gaps;
- silhouette variation.

Use multiple approved tree/vegetation variants where available.

Tree placement must support gameplay readability while still appearing
natural.

============================================================
GROUND DETAIL
============================================================

The ground must not feel empty.

Use restrained, coherent dressing such as:
- grasses;
- weeds;
- leaf/debris zones;
- small stones;
- branches;
- roots;
- dirt transitions;
- disturbed soil;
- rubble near structures;
- environmental storytelling.

Avoid uniform random scatter.

Ground dressing should form intentional clusters and environmental patterns.

============================================================
ROCKS
============================================================

Use rocks and boulders as part of the terrain language.

Use:
- isolated boulders;
- small clusters;
- exposed formations;
- slope reinforcement;
- path-edge rocks;
- landmark formations where appropriate.

Avoid repeating the exact same boulder at obvious identical scale/rotation.

Use approved boulder families and variations whenever possible.

============================================================
LANDMARKS
============================================================

The player needs memorable navigation landmarks.

Create a limited number of strong, believable landmarks rather than many
weak props.

Potential themes may include:
- abandoned infrastructure;
- ruined checkpoint;
- radio/communications structure;
- farm remnants;
- utility infrastructure;
- lookout;
- damaged road elements;
- industrial remnants;
- natural rock landmark;
- bunker approach structures.

Landmarks must help navigation and storytelling.

============================================================
BUNKER
============================================================

Preserve the intended bunker gameplay where required, but integrate the
facility naturally into the NEW landscape.

The bunker approach should create:
- anticipation;
- partial concealment;
- environmental storytelling;
- readable entrance;
- believable earth embedment;
- drainage/utilities;
- disturbed terrain;
- human infrastructure transitioning into wilderness.

Do not expose the whole bunker as an isolated object sitting in an empty
field.

============================================================
WILDLIFE
============================================================

Add restrained ambient wildlife where it improves believability.

Examples:
- distant deer;
- birds;
- occasional silhouettes;
- ambient movement.

Wildlife should:
- remain inexpensive;
- not dominate gameplay;
- avoid excessive AI/pathfinding;
- use pooling or low-cost behavior where appropriate;
- contribute to atmosphere.

============================================================
ATMOSPHERE
============================================================

Create strong environmental depth.

Review:
- Lighting;
- Atmosphere;
- fog;
- ColorCorrection;
- sky;
- sun direction;
- shadow readability;
- exterior lights;
- contrast;
- saturation;
- distance visibility.

The game should remain playable.

Darkness must create mood without making navigation impossible.

Use atmospheric perspective so the world reads as large.

============================================================
GAMEPLAY INTEGRATION
============================================================

The new visual world must preserve or correctly integrate canonical gameplay:

- player spawning;
- harvesting;
- trees/resources;
- inventory;
- scrap;
- farming;
- processor;
- bunker interactions;
- power;
- zombies;
- enemy spawning;
- objectives;
- navigation;
- day/night;
- survival loop.

Do not preserve bad visual composition merely because the gameplay anchors
currently exist there.

Move/reintegrate anchors safely when necessary while preserving their
contracts.

============================================================
SCRIPTS
============================================================

Add or modify scripts where genuinely required for the new environment.

Examples may include:
- ambient wildlife;
- environmental effects;
- optimized presentation logic;
- world behavior;
- integration fixes.

Maintain server authority where appropriate.

Do not add unnecessary per-object scripts.

Prefer centralized/pool-based systems.

============================================================
PERFORMANCE
============================================================

The world should look substantially richer without becoming technically
reckless.

Review:
- descendant count;
- BaseParts;
- MeshParts;
- collisions;
- anchored state;
- lights;
- shadow lights;
- particle emitters;
- sounds;
- pathfinding;
- streaming implications;
- duplicated geometry;
- expensive scripts.

Optimize intelligently rather than reducing the world to low visual quality.

============================================================
SAFE SOURCE-OF-TRUTH WORKFLOW
============================================================

Filesystem/Rojo remains the persistent source of truth for approved work.

BUT:

Rojo is NOT permission to destroy Studio state.

Before potentially destructive synchronization:
- inspect what will change;
- preserve recovery evidence;
- verify source completeness;
- verify the intended world root;
- ensure the rejected archived world is not accidentally promoted;
- ensure unrelated Studio work will not be destroyed.

Never perform a blind destructive sync.

Create physical recovery checkpoints during meaningful milestones.

